<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pointe-Noire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pointe-Noire is the second city and main seaport of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, on the Atlantic coast. It grew from the 1920s as the ocean terminus of the Congo-Ocean railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>, built at a heavy cost in lives of conscripted African workers and opened in 1934. Its deep-water port and the offshore oil fields discovered after independence have made it the country's economic centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pointe-noire.jpg" alt="Pointe-Noire" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArnoBOUJIKA · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wikim%C3%A9dia_R%C3%A9publique_du_Congo_-_Pointe_Noire._07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
